          "metadata": {
            "name": "Ingredient Variance"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "end"
          },
          "code": "INGREDIENT_VARIANCE",
          "id": "1775934575519",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1775934575520_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "From {{$context.$nodes.GET_EXECUTION_BATCH_INGREDIENT_VARIANCE.$output}}, extract the following fields for each record:\n- Process\n- Recipe\n- Batch\n- Ingredient\n- BatchPlannedIngredient\n- BatchActualIngredient\n- Deviation\n- PercentDeviation\n- UomCode\n\nGenerate a description in less than 100 words using a production supervisor or manufacturing engineer tone. Base it only on the records shown. Highlight what requires attention, including ingredient execution quantity variance, affected process/recipe/batch/ingredient, batch planned quantity versus batch actual quantity, deviation, percent deviation, and UOM. Provide actionable guidance to validate material consumption, check ingredient issue records, review execution transactions, and investigate operator or process variance drivers. Do not use phrases like \"the table\", \"below table\", \"displayed data\", \"below data\", or \"shown above\".\n\nGenerate a dynamic multiRecordWidget Subject:\n- If all records have `Deviation` and `PercentDeviation` equal to 0, null, or blank, set Subject as \"No Ingredient Execution Quantity Deviations\".\n- If any record has non-zero `Deviation` or non-zero `PercentDeviation`, set Subject as \"Ingredient Execution Quantity Deviations Requiring Attention\".\n- The Subject must be concise, specific, and aligned with the actual deviation status.\n\nDisplay the results using a multiRecordWidget with the following columns and exact order:\n\n1. Process → Process\n2. Recipe → Recipe\n3. Batch → Batch\n4. Ingredient → Ingredient\n5. BatchPlannedIngredient with UomCode → Batch Planned Quantity\n6. BatchActualIngredient with UomCode → Batch Actual Quantity\n7. Deviation → Deviation\n8. PercentDeviation → Deviation Percentage\n\nEnsure:\n- Each row represents one record from the source data.\n- Only the multiRecordWidget is returned as the output.\n- Set the multiRecordWidget Subject property using the dynamic Subject rule above.\n- Set the generated description as the multiRecordWidget `description` property.\n- The `description` must render below the Subject and above the column headers and records.\n- Do not render the description as footer text, trailing text, an extra row, or content after the records.\n- Do not display raw JSON, explanations, or instructions.\n- Do not include `UomCode` as a separate column.\n- Display `BatchPlannedIngredient` values with `UomCode` appended, for example `100 KG`.\n- Display `BatchActualIngredient` values with `UomCode` appended, for example `95 KG`.\n- Display PercentDeviation values with a `%` suffix, for example `12.5%`, `0%`, or `-8.75%`.\n- Preserve the exact column order specified above."
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "aiAppOutputSpecification": {
            "dataDisplay": {
              "layouts": [
                "ORA_LAYOUT_MULTIRECORD"
              ],
              "enableActions": false,
              "enableCommunications": false
            }
          },
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Product Variance"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "end"
          },
          "code": "PRODUCT_VARIANCE",
          "id": "1775934765637",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1775934765638_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "From {{$context.$nodes.GET_EXECUTION_BATCH_PRODUCT_VARIANCE.$output}}, extract the following fields for each record:\n- Process\n- Recipe\n- Batch\n- Product\n- BatchPlannedOutput\n- BatchActualOutput\n- Deviation\n- PercentDeviation\n- UomCode\n\nGenerate a description in less than 100 words using a production supervisor or manufacturing engineer tone. Base it only on the records shown. Highlight what requires attention, including product execution output variance, affected process/recipe/batch/product, batch planned output versus batch actual output, deviation, percent deviation, and UOM. Provide actionable guidance to validate batch output, check completion records, review yield reporting, and investigate production execution variance drivers. Do not use phrases like \"the table\", \"below table\", \"displayed data\", \"below data\", or \"shown above\".\n\nGenerate a dynamic multiRecordWidget Subject:\n- If all records have `Deviation` and `PercentDeviation` equal to 0, null, or blank, set Subject as \"No Product Execution Quantity Deviations\".\n- If any record has non-zero `Deviation` or non-zero `PercentDeviation`, set Subject as \"Product Execution Quantity Deviations Requiring Attention\".\n- The Subject must be concise, specific, and aligned with the actual deviation status.\n\nDisplay the results using a multiRecordWidget with the following columns and exact order:\n\n1. Process → Process\n2. Recipe → Recipe\n3. Batch → Batch\n4. Product → Product\n5. BatchPlannedOutput with UomCode → Batch Planned Output\n6. BatchActualOutput with UomCode → Batch Actual Output\n7. Deviation → Deviation\n8. PercentDeviation → Deviation Percentage\n\nEnsure:\n- Each row represents one record from the source data.\n- Only the multiRecordWidget is returned as the output.\n- Set the multiRecordWidget Subject property using the dynamic Subject rule above.\n- Set the generated description as the multiRecordWidget `description` property.\n- The `description` must render below the Subject and above the column headers and records.\n- Do not render the description as footer text, trailing text, an extra row, or content after the records.\n- Do not display raw JSON, explanations, or instructions.\n- Do not include `UomCode` as a separate column.\n- Display `BatchPlannedOutput` values with `UomCode` appended, for example `100 KG`.\n- Display `BatchActualOutput` values with `UomCode` appended, for example `95 KG`.\n- Display PercentDeviation values with a `%` suffix, for example `12.5%`, `0%`, or `-8.75%`.\n- Preserve the exact column order specified above."
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "aiAppOutputSpecification": {
            "dataDisplay": {
              "layouts": [
                "ORA_LAYOUT_MULTIRECORD"
              ],
              "enableActions": false,
              "enableCommunications": false
            }
          },
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Resource Variance"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "end"
          },
          "code": "RESOURCE_VARIANCE",
          "id": "1775935015890",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1775935015890_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "From {{$context.$nodes.GET_EXECUTION_BATCH_RESOURCE_VARIANCE.$output}}, extract the following fields for each record:\n- Process\n- Recipe\n- Batch\n- ResourceName\n- BatchPlannedResource\n- BatchActualResource\n- Deviation\n- PercentDeviation\n- UomCode\n\nGenerate a description in less than 100 words using a production supervisor or manufacturing engineer tone. Base it only on the records shown. Highlight what requires attention, including resource execution usage variance, affected process/recipe/batch/resource, batch planned usage versus batch actual usage, deviation, percent deviation, and UOM. Provide actionable guidance to validate labor or equipment usage, check execution transactions, review routing standards, and investigate resource utilization variance drivers. Do not use phrases like \"the table\", \"below table\", \"displayed data\", \"below data\", or \"shown above\".\n\nGenerate a dynamic multiRecordWidget Subject:\n- If all records have `Deviation` and `PercentDeviation` equal to 0, null, or blank, set Subject as \"No Resource Execution Usage Deviations\".\n- If any record has non-zero `Deviation` or non-zero `PercentDeviation`, set Subject as \"Resource Execution Usage Deviations Requiring Attention\".\n- The Subject must be concise, specific, and aligned with the actual deviation status.\n\nDisplay the results using a multiRecordWidget with the following columns and exact order:\n\n1. Process → Process\n2. Recipe → Recipe\n3. Batch → Batch\n4. ResourceName → Resource\n5. BatchPlannedResource with UomCode → Batch Planned Usage\n6. BatchActualResource with UomCode → Batch Actual Usage\n7. Deviation → Deviation\n8. PercentDeviation → Deviation Percentage\n\nEnsure:\n- Each row represents one record from the source data.\n- Only the multiRecordWidget is returned as the output.\n- Set the multiRecordWidget Subject property using the dynamic Subject rule above.\n- Set the generated description as the multiRecordWidget `description` property.\n- The `description` must render below the Subject and above the column headers and records.\n- Do not render the description as footer text, trailing text, an extra row, or content after the records.\n- Do not display raw JSON, explanations, or instructions.\n- Do not include `UomCode` as a separate column.\n- Display `BatchPlannedResource` values with `UomCode` appended, for example `100 HRS`.\n- Display `BatchActualResource` values with `UomCode` appended, for example `95 HRS`.\n- Display PercentDeviation values with a `%` suffix, for example `12.5%`, `0%`, or `-8.75%`.\n- Preserve the exact column order specified above."
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
